1
00:01:15,658 --> 00:01:18,994
All her life she had wanted
to teach at Wellesley College.

2
00:01:19,162 --> 00:01:22,206
So when a position opened
in the Art History department...

3
00:01:22,373 --> 00:01:26,126
...she pursued it single-mindedly
until she was hired.

4
00:01:31,841 --> 00:01:34,051
It was whispered
that Katherine Watson...

5
00:01:34,511 --> 00:01:36,762
...a first-year teacher
from Oakland State...

6
00:01:36,930 --> 00:01:40,182
...made up in brains
what she lacked in pedigree.

7
00:01:50,610 --> 00:01:53,278
Which was why this bohemian
from California...

8
00:01:53,446 --> 00:01:57,783
...was on her way to the most
conservative college in the nation.

9
00:01:57,951 --> 00:02:00,536
-Excuse me, please.
-Oh, sorry.

10
00:02:02,247 --> 00:02:03,914
Excuse me.

11
00:02:08,419 --> 00:02:10,254
Excuse me. The bus?

12
00:02:10,421 --> 00:02:12,131
-Keep walking, ma'am.
-Thank you.

13
00:02:12,298 --> 00:02:16,051
But Katherine Watson didn't come
to Wellesley to fit in.

14
00:02:16,594 --> 00:02:20,681
She came to Wellesley because
she wanted to make a difference.

15
00:02:20,849 --> 00:02:24,476
-Violet.
-My favorite Italian professor.

16
00:02:24,644 --> 00:02:26,603
-Nice summer?
-Terrific, thanks.

17
00:02:26,771 --> 00:02:28,939
-Who's that over there?
-Where?

18
00:02:29,107 --> 00:02:33,777
Oh, Katherine Watson. New teacher.
Art History. I'm dying to meet her.

19
00:03:35,215 --> 00:03:38,091
Who knocks at the Door of Learning?

20
00:03:38,259 --> 00:03:42,804
-I am every woman.
-What do you seek?

21
00:03:42,972 --> 00:03:45,599
To awaken my spirit
through hard work...

22
00:03:45,767 --> 00:03:48,018
...and dedicate my life
to knowledge.

23
00:03:48,186 --> 00:03:49,645
Then you are welcome.

24
00:03:49,812 --> 00:03:54,900
All women who seek to follow you
can enter here.

25
00:03:55,068 --> 00:03:59,821
I now declare
the academic year begun.

26
00:04:19,008 --> 00:04:23,553
A shame you didn't come yesterday.
It's so quiet before the girls arrive.

27
00:04:26,266 --> 00:04:29,643
Just a few rules.
No holes in the walls.

28
00:04:29,811 --> 00:04:34,439
No pets, no loud noises, no radio
or hi-fi after 8 on weekdays...

29
00:04:34,607 --> 00:04:36,316
...10 on weekends...

30
00:04:36,484 --> 00:04:39,945
...no hot plates and no male visitors.

31
00:04:42,365 --> 00:04:43,907
Anything wrong?

32
00:04:44,826 --> 00:04:48,370
I don't think I can go a year
without a hot plate.

33
00:04:52,500 --> 00:04:55,502
Don't you just love chintz?

34
00:04:55,670 --> 00:04:57,546
And look.

35
00:05:01,551 --> 00:05:03,385
They match.

36
00:05:03,553 --> 00:05:07,472
Sweet, right? Your room's here.

37
00:05:07,640 --> 00:05:11,685
My room is just across the way...

38
00:05:12,228 --> 00:05:14,938
...and Amanda Armstrong's
down the other end.

39
00:05:15,565 --> 00:05:18,233
-You grew up here?
-My whole life.

40
00:05:18,401 --> 00:05:22,237
You'll meet my parents
when they come to visit.

41
00:05:22,405 --> 00:05:25,741
-They visit?
-Regularly.

42
00:05:28,244 --> 00:05:33,915
-What do you teach?
-Speech, elocution and poise.

43
00:05:34,334 --> 00:05:36,752
Dinners are communal,
so I'll handle that.

44
00:05:36,919 --> 00:05:40,547
But breakfast and lunch,
you're on your own. So...

45
00:05:40,715 --> 00:05:45,761
...we each get our own shelf.
I'll make your label this evening.

46
00:05:46,220 --> 00:05:52,142
I don't need to tell you, everything on
our individual shelves is sacrosanct.

47
00:05:53,978 --> 00:05:57,647
I just knew when we met...

48
00:05:58,566 --> 00:06:00,942
...we'd be instant friends.

49
00:06:19,128 --> 00:06:23,673
Be careful. They can smell fear.

50
00:06:43,986 --> 00:06:46,822
-Good morning.
-Good morning.

51
00:06:47,156 --> 00:06:48,824
Thank you.

52
00:06:49,575 --> 00:06:54,454
This is History of Art 100.

53
00:06:54,997 --> 00:06:59,042
We'll be following
Dr. Staunton's syllabus.

54
00:06:59,210 --> 00:07:02,170
-Any questions so far?
-Your name?

55
00:07:03,172 --> 00:07:05,549
-Why don't you go first?
-Connie Baker.

56
00:07:05,716 --> 00:07:10,387
-Katherine Watson. Nice to meet you.
-Dr. Watson, I presume.

57
00:07:11,514 --> 00:07:14,724
-Not yet. And you are?
-Giselle Levy.

58
00:07:14,892 --> 00:07:17,477
Giselle. If someone could get the--

59
00:07:18,729 --> 00:07:22,691
-Susan Delacorte.
-Thank you, Susan Delacorte.

60
00:07:26,195 --> 00:07:31,116
From the beginning, man has always
had the impulse to create art.

61
00:07:31,284 --> 00:07:34,703
-Can anyone tell me what this is?
-Wounded Bison, Altamira, Spain...

62
00:07:34,871 --> 00:07:38,165
...about 15,000 B.C.
Joan Brandwyn.

63
00:07:38,624 --> 00:07:40,542
Very good, Joan.

64
00:07:40,877 --> 00:07:44,880
Despite the age of these, they are
technically sophisticated because--

65
00:07:45,047 --> 00:07:49,551
The shading and the thickness of the
lines moving over the bison's hump.

66
00:07:49,719 --> 00:07:53,680
-Is that right?
-Yes, that's exactly right.

67
00:07:54,432 --> 00:07:56,433
Next slide.

68
00:07:56,976 --> 00:08:01,062
This is probably less familiar.
It was discovered by archeologists--

69
00:08:01,230 --> 00:08:05,192
In 1879, Lascaux, France.
Dates back to 10,000 B.C.

70
00:08:05,359 --> 00:08:09,654
Singled out because of flowing lines
depicting the movement of the animal.

71
00:08:11,782 --> 00:08:14,534
-Impressive. Name?
-Herd of Horses.

72
00:08:14,702 --> 00:08:17,913
-I meant yours.
-We call her Flicka.

73
00:08:18,539 --> 00:08:21,291
Elizabeth Warren.
They call me Betty.

74
00:08:21,459 --> 00:08:23,084
Very good.

75
00:08:23,252 --> 00:08:25,921
Betty is also correct.

76
00:08:26,088 --> 00:08:29,966
Just because something is ancient
doesn't mean that it is primitive.

77
00:08:30,134 --> 00:08:32,385
For example. Next slide, please.

78
00:08:32,553 --> 00:08:35,055
Mycerinus and His Queen. 2470 B.C.

79
00:08:35,223 --> 00:08:37,724
It's a funerary statue
of the pharaoh and queen...

80
00:08:37,892 --> 00:08:42,187
...originally intended to preserve
the pharaoh's ka. Soul.

81
00:08:45,775 --> 00:08:48,693
Have any of you taken
Art History before?

82
00:08:48,861 --> 00:08:51,112
No.

83
00:08:53,824 --> 00:08:56,451
Let's go on. Slide.

84
00:08:56,786 --> 00:08:59,704
Seated Scribe. Egypt. 2400 B.C.

85
00:09:00,623 --> 00:09:04,292
Peasant Couple Plowing.
Sixteenth century B.C. Egypt.

86
00:09:04,919 --> 00:09:08,004
Snake Goddess.
Minoan. 1600 B.C.

87
00:09:08,798 --> 00:09:10,715
Fresco. Minoan. 1600 B.C.

88
00:09:11,634 --> 00:09:13,802
Funeral Mask. Mycenaean. 1200.

89
00:09:26,148 --> 00:09:27,524
Could someone please get--?

90
00:09:34,323 --> 00:09:35,991
Thank you.

91
00:09:37,535 --> 00:09:40,954
By a show of hands only...

92
00:09:41,789 --> 00:09:45,959
...how many of you have read
the entire text?

93
00:09:48,421 --> 00:09:53,800
-And the suggested supplements.
-Long way from Oakland State?

94
00:09:57,221 --> 00:09:59,681
Well, you girls do prepare.

95
00:09:59,849 --> 00:10:03,602
If you've nothing else for us,
we could go to independent study.

96
00:10:04,520 --> 00:10:06,146
Ac--

97
00:10:42,892 --> 00:10:49,022
I was in California once. How do you
get work done with all that sunshine?

98
00:10:49,357 --> 00:10:52,984
-We tan in class.
-Really?

99
00:10:53,235 --> 00:10:54,569
No.

100
00:10:58,866 --> 00:11:01,451
You know, not everybody
wanted you.

101
00:11:01,619 --> 00:11:04,120
I'm not naming any names.

102
00:11:04,288 --> 00:11:08,917
These jobs usually go quickly.
Ex-students, friends of, you know...

103
00:11:09,085 --> 00:11:11,419
...the right people.

104
00:11:11,587 --> 00:11:15,507
The person they wanted took a job at
Brown, and no one else was available.

105
00:11:15,675 --> 00:11:17,759
So here you are.

106
00:11:18,594 --> 00:11:22,430
You can go in now. Good luck.

107
00:11:28,437 --> 00:11:31,231
Your first class left a lot
to be desired, Miss Watson.

108
00:11:31,399 --> 00:11:35,068
And I'm curious about the subject
of your dissertation.

109
00:11:35,236 --> 00:11:40,156
You suggest, "Picasso will do
for the 20th century...

110
00:11:40,324 --> 00:11:44,285
...what Michelangelo did
for the Renaissance," unquote.

111
00:11:45,037 --> 00:11:47,497
In terms of influencing movements.

112
00:11:47,665 --> 00:11:50,875
So these canvases that
they're turning out these days...

113
00:11:51,043 --> 00:11:54,129
...with paint dripped
and splotched on them...

114
00:11:54,296 --> 00:11:59,259
...they're as worthy of our attention
as Michelangelo's Sistine Chapel?

115
00:11:59,802 --> 00:12:01,594
I'm not comparing them.

116
00:12:01,762 --> 00:12:05,390
Have you ever seen
the Sistine Chapel, Miss Watson?

117
00:12:05,558 --> 00:12:07,559
Actually stood there?

118
00:12:09,186 --> 00:12:11,062
I've never been to Europe.

119
00:12:14,316 --> 00:12:19,154
I can assure all of you this is the place
I want to be more than anything.

120
00:12:19,321 --> 00:12:23,825
Better discipline next class,
Miss Watson.

121
00:12:29,165 --> 00:12:31,374
-Hello.
-It's me.

122
00:12:31,876 --> 00:12:34,586
-Hey!
-Collect from Katherine Watson.

123
00:12:34,754 --> 00:12:38,339
-Will you accept?
-Yeah, sure, of course I will.

124
00:12:38,966 --> 00:12:41,176
Hey, is everything okay?

125
00:12:42,428 --> 00:12:43,428
Yeah.

126
00:12:44,722 --> 00:12:46,347
Tough, huh?

127
00:12:49,435 --> 00:12:51,936
Well, how are the classes?

128
00:12:52,563 --> 00:12:54,522
Snobs, right?

129
00:12:55,816 --> 00:12:59,903
-I hate to say I told you so.
-You don't have to.

130
00:13:06,452 --> 00:13:08,578
I can't really talk right now.

131
00:13:09,789 --> 00:13:11,706
I'll write you tonight.

132
00:13:15,878 --> 00:13:17,378
So you got a fella?

133
00:13:18,839 --> 00:13:22,050
He's-- He's there. I'm here.

134
00:13:22,218 --> 00:13:27,722
Long distance. Torture. I know.
Come. Come in and sit down.

135
00:13:31,393 --> 00:13:35,563
When Lenny left for the South Pacific,
it nearly broke my heart.

136
00:13:35,731 --> 00:13:38,441
We wrote every day until....

137
00:13:41,111 --> 00:13:43,238
He was a great man.

138
00:13:44,031 --> 00:13:46,950
-I'm sorry.
-It was a hundred years ago.

139
00:13:47,117 --> 00:13:48,910
I'm babbling.

140
00:13:51,163 --> 00:13:56,209
I love Lucy.
Even if she is a communist.

141
00:13:56,377 --> 00:13:59,254
The only thing red about Lucy
is her hair.

142
00:13:59,421 --> 00:14:01,881
And even that's fake. Desi said it.

143
00:14:02,049 --> 00:14:03,967
Winchell wrote it.

144
00:14:04,760 --> 00:14:07,679
Amanda Armstrong.
I see you survived.

145
00:14:07,847 --> 00:14:09,722
Katherine Watson. Just barely.

146
00:14:09,890 --> 00:14:13,017
Oh, good. You've met.
Katherine's taken the third bedroom.

147
00:14:13,185 --> 00:14:16,187
How about a little dinner
before What's My Line?

148
00:14:17,231 --> 00:14:19,774
How about a little drink?

149
00:14:22,111 --> 00:14:24,946
Her companion died in May.

150
00:14:25,489 --> 00:14:26,823
Companion?

151
00:14:27,658 --> 00:14:30,034
You know, companion.

152
00:14:31,161 --> 00:14:37,166
Josephine Burns.
Taught biology here for 30 years.

153
00:14:40,546 --> 00:14:43,673
You'll love it here, Katherine.
You'll see.

154
00:14:43,841 --> 00:14:48,803
I already do. I-- Honestly,
it's beautiful. It's perfect, really.

155
00:14:48,971 --> 00:14:53,516
Well, don't fool yourself. They have
claws underneath their white gloves.

156
00:14:54,810 --> 00:14:56,311
Who?

157
00:14:57,104 --> 00:15:01,649
The alumnae, their offspring,
the faculty. You name it.

158
00:15:01,984 --> 00:15:03,818
Watch out for yourself.

159
00:15:03,986 --> 00:15:06,029
Too much independence
frightens them.

160
00:15:06,196 --> 00:15:09,991
-Will you please stop?
-Oh, a word of advice.

161
00:15:10,159 --> 00:15:13,536
Don't let those girls
know that they got to you.

162
00:15:16,040 --> 00:15:19,125
-They didn't.
-Good for you.

163
00:15:20,586 --> 00:15:22,837
You almost convinced me.

164
00:15:53,202 --> 00:15:56,120
-What is that?
-You tell me.

165
00:15:59,541 --> 00:16:03,378
Carcass by Soutine. 1925.

166
00:16:04,338 --> 00:16:07,840
-It's not on the syllabus.
-No, it's not.

167
00:16:09,843 --> 00:16:12,095
Is it any good?

168
00:16:17,351 --> 00:16:21,980
Come on, ladies.
There's no wrong answer.

169
00:16:22,147 --> 00:16:25,650
There's also no textbook
telling you what to think.

170
00:16:26,652 --> 00:16:28,736
It's not that easy, is it?

171
00:16:29,822 --> 00:16:32,156
All right. No. It's not good.

172
00:16:32,324 --> 00:16:35,118
In fact, I wouldn't even call it art.
It's grotesque.

173
00:16:35,327 --> 00:16:37,203
Is there a rule against
grotesque art?

174
00:16:37,413 --> 00:16:40,873
I think there's something
aggressive about it...

175
00:16:41,583 --> 00:16:44,752
-...and erotic.
-To you, everything is erotic.

176
00:16:44,920 --> 00:16:47,338
-Everything is erotic.
-Girls.

177
00:16:47,506 --> 00:16:49,757
-Aren't there standards?
-Of course.

178
00:16:49,925 --> 00:16:53,261
Otherwise a tacky velvet painting
could be equated to a Rembrandt.

179
00:16:53,429 --> 00:16:57,640
My Uncle Ferdie has two tacky velvet
paintings. He loves those clowns.

180
00:16:57,808 --> 00:17:03,563
There are standards, technique,
composition, color, even subject.

181
00:17:03,731 --> 00:17:06,733
So if you're suggesting
that rotted side of meat is art...

182
00:17:06,900 --> 00:17:10,069
...much less good art,
then what are we going to learn?

183
00:17:11,238 --> 00:17:12,780
Just that.

184
00:17:12,948 --> 00:17:16,534
You have outlined our new syllabus,
Betty. Thank you.

185
00:17:16,702 --> 00:17:19,620
What is art?
What makes it good or bad?

186
00:17:19,788 --> 00:17:21,748
And who decides?

187
00:17:22,708 --> 00:17:24,167
Next slide, please.

188
00:17:26,211 --> 00:17:29,297
Twenty-five years ago someone
thought this was brilliant.

189
00:17:29,465 --> 00:17:31,716
-I can see that.
-Who?

190
00:17:31,884 --> 00:17:35,428
My mother. I painted it
for her birthday.

191
00:17:35,596 --> 00:17:39,807
Next slide. This is my mom.

192
00:17:40,392 --> 00:17:43,686
-Is it art?
-It's a snapshot.

193
00:17:43,854 --> 00:17:47,815
If I told you Ansel Adams had taken it,
would that make a difference?

194
00:17:48,859 --> 00:17:53,279
-Art isn't art until someone says it is.
-It's art!

195
00:17:54,531 --> 00:17:57,283
-The right people.
-Who are they?

196
00:17:57,493 --> 00:17:58,826
Betty Warren!

197
00:17:58,994 --> 00:18:02,580
-We're lucky we have one right here.
-Screw you.

198
00:18:04,374 --> 00:18:06,751
Could you go back
to the Soutine, please?

199
00:18:09,088 --> 00:18:13,132
Just look at it again.
Look beyond the paint.

200
00:18:13,300 --> 00:18:18,179
Let us try to open our minds
to a new idea.

201
00:18:21,100 --> 00:18:25,186
All right, back to chapter three.
Has anyone read it?

202
00:18:27,439 --> 00:18:28,481
Okay.

203
00:18:31,860 --> 00:18:34,821
"When your courses are set,
and a dream boat you've met...

204
00:18:34,988 --> 00:18:37,031
...have a real cigarette.
Have a Camel."

205
00:18:37,199 --> 00:18:41,202
I've got my courses, I've got my Camel
cigarette. Where is my dream boat?

206
00:18:41,370 --> 00:18:44,914
-Giselle, where is my dream boat?
-Betty's cousin isn't good enough?

207
00:18:45,082 --> 00:18:47,125
-I haven't met him.
-Don't encourage her.

208
00:18:47,292 --> 00:18:49,877
He's only escorting
Connie as a favor.

209
00:18:50,045 --> 00:18:51,963
Why are you like this?

210
00:18:52,631 --> 00:18:54,715
I didn't mean that.

211
00:18:54,883 --> 00:18:57,552
I'm just under so much pressure
with the wedding.

212
00:18:57,719 --> 00:19:00,721
Do you realize November 2nd
is three weeks from now?

213
00:19:00,889 --> 00:19:02,807
-Oh, honey.
-Don't have it.

214
00:19:02,975 --> 00:19:04,600
-Don't come.
-Here. Here.

215
00:19:04,768 --> 00:19:07,979
I'm working on table seating now,
so I can just erase your name.

216
00:19:08,147 --> 00:19:09,397
-Can I see it?
-No.

217
00:19:09,565 --> 00:19:11,607
Let me look.
I can't look for a second?

218
00:19:11,775 --> 00:19:15,194
-You want to see where Bill is sitting.
-No. That is over. Right?

219
00:19:15,362 --> 00:19:18,030
Right? Giselle, right?

220
00:19:20,033 --> 00:19:21,576
Damn it.

221
00:19:27,583 --> 00:19:31,085
-Do I look a little bit like her?
-Like who?

222
00:19:31,587 --> 00:19:35,047
-Katherine Watson.
-You mean, "crap is art"?

223
00:19:35,757 --> 00:19:40,052
-I think she's fabulous.
-Well, no man wanted her.

224
00:19:41,430 --> 00:19:43,598
-She isn't dead.
-She's at least 30.

225
00:19:43,765 --> 00:19:46,851
-Oh, no. No.
-I guess she never wanted children.

226
00:19:47,019 --> 00:19:48,936
For your information...

227
00:19:49,104 --> 00:19:55,151
...Katherine Watson had to take
this job to escape from California.

228
00:19:55,861 --> 00:19:57,111
Please.

229
00:19:57,279 --> 00:20:01,199
She had a torrid affair
with a Hollywood movie star.

230
00:20:01,366 --> 00:20:05,703
-She came here to get away.
-That's ridiculous.

231
00:20:05,871 --> 00:20:08,623
-Who was it?
-I don't know.

232
00:20:09,082 --> 00:20:11,667
Who was it, Giselle?
Don't be a pimple! Tell me!

233
00:20:11,835 --> 00:20:14,545
-It's ridiculous.
-Tell me. No, it's not. You know.

234
00:20:14,713 --> 00:20:18,341
-You know something. Tell me.
-It's William Holden.

235
00:20:19,801 --> 00:20:21,552
-Fantastic!
-I know.

236
00:20:21,720 --> 00:20:23,346
Who is it?

237
00:20:24,264 --> 00:20:26,724
William Holden.

238
00:20:35,275 --> 00:20:39,445
I know. I know. I'm late. I'm-- Abject
pleading, apologies, forgiveness.

239
00:20:39,738 --> 00:20:41,280
