479
00:39:25,613 --> 00:39:27,822
She's too smart for him.

480
00:39:27,990 --> 00:39:29,282
Hold that.

481
00:39:40,961 --> 00:39:43,046
-They're playing our song.
-What?

482
00:39:43,214 --> 00:39:46,466
-They're playing our song.
-I heard you.

483
00:39:46,634 --> 00:39:49,677
-Hi.
-Hi, Giselle.

484
00:39:52,306 --> 00:39:54,182
-Ladies' choice?
-Sure.

485
00:39:55,476 --> 00:39:57,102
Excuse me.

486
00:40:05,361 --> 00:40:08,988
Ladies, gentlemen! Quiet!

487
00:40:09,740 --> 00:40:12,242
What's this about the poem
you wanted me to read?

488
00:40:12,410 --> 00:40:15,412
-It was your idea, remember?
-I never said that.

489
00:40:15,579 --> 00:40:17,122
Ready?

490
00:40:18,916 --> 00:40:19,916
Excellent.

491
00:40:20,751 --> 00:40:25,171
Well, I wrote this down, just in case
you forgot. It's my favorite.

492
00:40:25,923 --> 00:40:27,507
Smile.

493
00:40:33,556 --> 00:40:38,268
Now, Betty, I tried to think of
a million ways to tell you how I feel.

494
00:40:38,436 --> 00:40:40,520
But instead...

495
00:40:41,313 --> 00:40:44,023
...I refer to your favorite poem.

496
00:40:53,784 --> 00:40:56,536
-Good catch.
-You said it.

497
00:40:58,789 --> 00:41:00,957
He's a morsel. Miss Watson!

498
00:41:01,125 --> 00:41:03,835
-He's a favor. Remember?
-Come on.

499
00:41:04,003 --> 00:41:07,005
Miss Watson,
I want you to meet Tommy.

500
00:41:07,173 --> 00:41:09,299
Tommy, come here for a second.

501
00:41:10,843 --> 00:41:13,428
You are so beautiful.

502
00:41:14,013 --> 00:41:17,765
-This is Katherine Watson.
-Oh, wow! In the flesh.

503
00:41:17,933 --> 00:41:21,644
-She has not shut up about you.
-Oh, stop.

504
00:41:21,812 --> 00:41:24,397
-You know what I'm saying?
-I do.

505
00:41:24,565 --> 00:41:28,318
-You did something to impress her.
-Well, it seems you did too.

506
00:41:31,113 --> 00:41:35,575
-You make these especially well.
-Thank you.

507
00:41:36,327 --> 00:41:39,162
I had a fella. Lenny.

508
00:41:40,247 --> 00:41:44,792
You remind me of him so much.

509
00:41:46,295 --> 00:41:48,087
He had this...

510
00:41:48,631 --> 00:41:51,716
...funny little thing
where his two front teeth...

511
00:41:51,884 --> 00:41:53,927
...they overlapped.

512
00:41:56,764 --> 00:41:58,389
He's dead.

513
00:42:01,268 --> 00:42:02,810
South Pacific.

514
00:42:22,831 --> 00:42:25,959
My parents say my future
is right on the horizon.

515
00:42:26,126 --> 00:42:28,503
Tell them the horizon
is an imaginary line...

516
00:42:28,671 --> 00:42:30,755
...that recedes as you approach it.

517
00:42:33,551 --> 00:42:36,135
I think I'll go back to my seat.

518
00:42:36,303 --> 00:42:39,305
I had a really nice time. Thank you.

519
00:42:40,808 --> 00:42:42,141
Connie.

520
00:42:43,102 --> 00:42:45,937
-Is this the brushoff?
-No.

521
00:42:46,105 --> 00:42:48,481
I thought you were, you know...

522
00:42:48,649 --> 00:42:50,608
...done with me.

523
00:42:52,027 --> 00:42:56,489
-Why would you think that?
-Betty said that you--

524
00:42:56,657 --> 00:43:02,036
I don't wanna take advantage.
I know that this was some favor.

525
00:43:02,204 --> 00:43:05,498
She did me the favor, not you.

526
00:43:06,417 --> 00:43:08,585
Come on, dance with me.

527
00:43:38,657 --> 00:43:40,950
She's too good for you.

528
00:43:42,536 --> 00:43:44,287
Maybe you're right.

529
00:43:45,873 --> 00:43:48,708
I'm too good for you too.

530
00:43:49,668 --> 00:43:54,714
-But I have lower expectations.
-I thought we settled this last spring.

531
00:43:55,090 --> 00:43:59,469
So then we probably shouldn't have
slept together over the summer.

532
00:43:59,803 --> 00:44:01,304
Stop.

533
00:44:04,767 --> 00:44:06,309
You ready?

534
00:44:06,727 --> 00:44:09,646
Do you wanna know something funny?

535
00:44:09,813 --> 00:44:12,398
Lenny's not dead.

536
00:44:12,858 --> 00:44:15,526
Not technically.

537
00:44:15,694 --> 00:44:16,986
He....

538
00:44:17,154 --> 00:44:18,655
He's got married.

539
00:44:18,822 --> 00:44:22,075
He's got a wife and kids...

540
00:44:22,618 --> 00:44:24,160
...and a mortgage.

541
00:44:24,328 --> 00:44:28,081
It was all supposed to be mine,
except for the wife.

542
00:44:30,000 --> 00:44:33,920
And you....
You don't look a thing like him.

543
00:44:34,088 --> 00:44:35,880
-You ugly bartender.
-Stop it.

544
00:44:36,048 --> 00:44:39,842
-You couldn't shine his shoes.
-I'm sorry.

545
00:44:40,010 --> 00:44:46,182
It wasn't supposed
to turn out like this.

546
00:44:47,226 --> 00:44:50,228
-Which way?
-Let me just see here.

547
00:44:50,396 --> 00:44:52,313
I'm not sure.

548
00:44:52,898 --> 00:44:56,150
-Where are we supposed to go?
-I think it's here.

549
00:44:56,318 --> 00:44:58,569
Come this way. We're almost there.

550
00:45:12,584 --> 00:45:13,918
Joe.

551
00:45:14,503 --> 00:45:16,921
-Hello.
-Hi. How are you?

552
00:45:17,089 --> 00:45:19,465
Great. Thank you so much for this.
You're a pal.

553
00:45:19,633 --> 00:45:21,718
It's a pleasure.
Your timing is perfect.

554
00:45:24,054 --> 00:45:25,930
There it is.

555
00:45:58,797 --> 00:46:02,008
-That's Jackson Pollock.
-In a word.

556
00:46:02,176 --> 00:46:06,637
I was getting used to the idea of dead,
maggoty meat being art, now this.

557
00:46:07,598 --> 00:46:10,641
Please don't tell me
we have to write a paper about it.

558
00:46:10,809 --> 00:46:12,226
Do me a favor.

559
00:46:12,394 --> 00:46:15,813
Do yourselves a favor.
Stop talking and look.

560
00:46:16,398 --> 00:46:21,444
You're not required to write a paper.
You're not even required to like it.

561
00:46:21,612 --> 00:46:23,863
You are required...

562
00:46:24,031 --> 00:46:25,740
...to consider it.

563
00:46:25,908 --> 00:46:30,620
That's your only assignment today.
When you're done, you may leave.

564
00:46:33,040 --> 00:46:35,708
Thank God Betty isn't here.

565
00:47:31,598 --> 00:47:33,641
Where did you come from?

566
00:47:33,809 --> 00:47:35,434
Mars.

567
00:47:36,687 --> 00:47:38,855
What a coincidence.

568
00:47:39,857 --> 00:47:45,820
Tom Donegal came by to see Joan.
So I hopped a ride.

569
00:47:46,196 --> 00:47:47,864
Why?

570
00:47:50,409 --> 00:47:52,493
So I could do this:

571
00:48:08,594 --> 00:48:10,803
-Katherine.
-Yes?

572
00:48:11,471 --> 00:48:13,431
-Have you got a minute?
-Of course.

573
00:48:13,599 --> 00:48:15,141
Walk with me.

574
00:48:15,934 --> 00:48:18,311
-You going home for the holidays?
-No.

575
00:48:18,478 --> 00:48:20,271
-Too far?
-Too expensive.

576
00:48:20,439 --> 00:48:22,982
But I've never had
a New England Christmas, so....

577
00:48:23,150 --> 00:48:26,861
-Our weather hasn't scared you away?
-I love it here.

578
00:48:27,029 --> 00:48:28,362
Do you?

579
00:48:28,739 --> 00:48:32,700
I've been getting some calls about
your teaching methods, Katherine.

580
00:48:32,868 --> 00:48:36,370
They're a little unorthodox...

581
00:48:36,538 --> 00:48:38,664
...for Wellesley.

582
00:48:38,832 --> 00:48:42,168
-We are traditionalists, Katherine.
-Yes, I noticed.

583
00:48:42,336 --> 00:48:44,837
So if you'd like to stay here....

584
00:48:45,005 --> 00:48:48,174
-Is that a question?
-More a discussion.

585
00:48:48,342 --> 00:48:52,720
-About my staying here?
-You'll have your formal review in May.

586
00:48:53,430 --> 00:48:55,389
Until then...

587
00:48:55,933 --> 00:48:59,185
...a little less modern art.

588
00:48:59,353 --> 00:49:01,270
Happy holidays.

589
00:49:01,438 --> 00:49:03,064
And to you.

590
00:49:09,196 --> 00:49:11,614
-Afternoon.
-Oh, hi--

591
00:49:16,411 --> 00:49:18,871
I hope you get that ring!

592
00:49:19,206 --> 00:49:22,875
Bye, girls, see you next year.
Have a safe trip.

593
00:49:30,968 --> 00:49:34,053
So, what's the big hush-hush secret?

594
00:49:34,221 --> 00:49:37,598
Are we gonna hear the pitter-patter
of little Spencers?

595
00:49:37,766 --> 00:49:40,017
Stop. They'll tell us
when they're ready.

596
00:49:40,185 --> 00:49:41,811
Are you ready?

597
00:49:41,979 --> 00:49:45,690
Is she the cutest? You are the cutest.
Come here, you.

598
00:49:50,570 --> 00:49:53,572
Spencer's been made junior partner.

599
00:49:54,408 --> 00:49:58,536
-It is about time.
-And it's a significant raise too!

600
00:49:58,704 --> 00:50:01,664
-Betty!
-What? I can say that, can't I?

601
00:50:01,832 --> 00:50:03,749
To Tommy and Joan?

602
00:50:03,917 --> 00:50:09,338
Well, it is.
So maybe a family isn't far behind.

603
00:50:10,424 --> 00:50:13,092
Let's take our coffee
in the sitting room, shall we?

604
00:50:13,260 --> 00:50:14,260
I'll help.

605
00:50:17,889 --> 00:50:21,058
-Keep them closed.
-All right.

606
00:50:25,939 --> 00:50:29,275
Beautiful! You've got everything
you've ever dreamed of.

607
00:50:29,443 --> 00:50:31,235
You will too.

608
00:50:32,279 --> 00:50:36,615
I've got a secret to tell you.
You swear you won't gab to anyone?

609
00:50:37,200 --> 00:50:40,327
I got accepted early
to Yale Law School.

610
00:50:40,495 --> 00:50:44,915
To what?! Why?!
You don't want to be a lawyer.

611
00:50:45,083 --> 00:50:46,834
Maybe I do.

612
00:50:47,127 --> 00:50:51,380
You won't switch cold creams without
asking me, but you applied to school?

613
00:50:51,548 --> 00:50:54,300
On a lark.
We never thought I'd get in.

614
00:50:54,468 --> 00:50:56,469
-Who's "we"?
-Miss Watson.

615
00:50:56,636 --> 00:50:59,805
She practically filled out
my application for me.

616
00:51:00,390 --> 00:51:03,309
You've got to be kidding me!
What right does she have?

617
00:51:03,477 --> 00:51:05,102
You're getting married!

618
00:51:05,270 --> 00:51:08,147
First of all,
there's no ring on this finger.

619
00:51:08,315 --> 00:51:11,817
Second, I can do both. I can.

620
00:51:12,319 --> 00:51:15,696
-How does Tommy feel about this?
-He doesn't know. No one does.

621
00:51:15,864 --> 00:51:18,908
-Not even her?
-No one.

622
00:51:19,076 --> 00:51:20,618
Joanie! Betty!

623
00:51:20,786 --> 00:51:23,746
You are this close to getting
everything you ever wanted.

624
00:51:23,914 --> 00:51:25,998
And this close to losing it.

625
00:51:37,844 --> 00:51:40,513
I just got a call.
They need me in New York tomorrow.

626
00:51:40,680 --> 00:51:43,474
Joan and Tommy are here.
Can't you leave in the morning?

627
00:51:43,642 --> 00:51:47,520
Then I'd miss the meeting.
Sorry, guys. We take a rain check?

628
00:51:47,687 --> 00:51:49,980
-Sure, buddy.
-We'll see you in the new year.

629
00:51:50,148 --> 00:51:52,024
Happy and merry.

630
00:51:55,529 --> 00:51:57,530
I'll call you in the morning.

631
00:52:10,627 --> 00:52:12,211
Coffee?

632
00:52:17,843 --> 00:52:20,719
Guess who has
an early Christmas present?

633
00:52:20,887 --> 00:52:24,557
-Oh, my! How can you live like this?
-Like what?

634
00:52:24,724 --> 00:52:27,059
We'll just wait for you
to tidy up a bit.

635
00:52:27,227 --> 00:52:31,063
In this lifetime? It'll never happen.
Come here, beautiful.

636
00:52:34,734 --> 00:52:37,611
What are you doing here?

637
00:52:37,988 --> 00:52:42,575
-You still like it here?
-I do. I like getting to know the girls.

638
00:52:42,742 --> 00:52:44,827
Most of the time.

639
00:52:44,995 --> 00:52:47,538
-And the weather.
-Yeah, the weather's nice.

640
00:52:47,706 --> 00:52:49,206
-Are you freezing?
-Yeah.

641
00:52:49,374 --> 00:52:52,084
-Your lips are a little blue.
-I like them that way.

642
00:52:52,252 --> 00:52:53,669
Why didn't you bring a coat?

643
00:53:18,904 --> 00:53:20,279
Bill.

644
00:53:21,990 --> 00:53:24,241
Giselle, what are you doing here?

645
00:53:25,452 --> 00:53:27,119
Freezing.

646
00:53:30,498 --> 00:53:34,627
Look, it's over.
I can't see you anymore.

647
00:53:34,794 --> 00:53:37,129
-I can't.
-I know.

648
00:53:38,632 --> 00:53:40,090
Really.

649
00:53:42,802 --> 00:53:45,221
I just want to talk to you
for a minute.

650
00:53:47,599 --> 00:53:49,183
Come on.

651
00:53:58,109 --> 00:53:59,818
Oh, I missed you.

652
00:54:01,321 --> 00:54:02,947
You're beautiful.

653
00:54:03,782 --> 00:54:05,658
Life without you...

654
00:54:05,825 --> 00:54:07,743
...just isn't life.

655
00:54:09,496 --> 00:54:12,790
And I don't wanna go through life
not living. Do you?

656
00:54:13,250 --> 00:54:14,667
No.

657
00:54:14,876 --> 00:54:20,005
I love you so much I would move
to this elitist icebox if you want me to.

658
00:54:25,845 --> 00:54:28,222
Make an honest man out of me.

659
00:54:29,849 --> 00:54:31,684
Miss Watson?

660
00:54:32,060 --> 00:54:34,853
Hi. I didn't know you were here.

661
00:54:35,855 --> 00:54:37,564
Hi.

662
00:54:39,442 --> 00:54:43,404
Giselle Levy, Bill Dunbar,
this is....

663
00:54:43,571 --> 00:54:45,823
-This is my.... Paul.
-Fiancé.

664
00:54:46,241 --> 00:54:49,243
As of about a minute and a half ago.
Paul Moore.

665
00:54:51,621 --> 00:54:55,749
God. Congratulations!
Your fiancé?

666
00:54:57,627 --> 00:54:59,420
You must be thrilled.

667
00:54:59,587 --> 00:55:02,047
I don't think she's caught
her breath yet.

668
00:55:02,215 --> 00:55:03,382
I haven't.

669
00:55:06,219 --> 00:55:10,764
-Well, sit down. We'll have a toast.
-We'd love to. We're in a hurry.

670
00:55:10,932 --> 00:55:14,059
But that's really great.
Congratulations, old man.

671
00:55:14,227 --> 00:55:17,521
-Congratulations. Merry Christmas.
-Come on, let's....

672
00:55:17,689 --> 00:55:21,233
Best of tidings to you both. Bye.

673
00:55:49,721 --> 00:55:51,263
No, Paul.

674
00:55:51,431 --> 00:55:54,183
Nancy'll have a heart attack.
She has these rules.

675
00:55:54,351 --> 00:55:56,769
-Come on.
-No.

676
00:55:57,479 --> 00:56:01,190
-Why?
-I said no. I'm not comfortable.

677
00:56:02,442 --> 00:56:05,152
I'll make up the convertible sofa.

678
00:56:12,369 --> 00:56:14,286
What are you doing?

679
00:56:16,790 --> 00:56:18,791
You're not kidding?

680
00:56:18,958 --> 00:56:24,421
I just came 3000 miles to see you.
I'm sleeping down here by myself?

681
00:56:28,259 --> 00:56:30,803
Stop. Stop. Stop for a second.

682
00:56:30,970 --> 00:56:34,640
-Tell me what the hell's going on.
-I don't know.

683
00:56:34,808 --> 00:56:37,226
I feel like everything
is getting away from me.

684
00:56:37,394 --> 00:56:42,481
We haven't actually, literally looked
at each other for three months...

685
00:56:42,649 --> 00:56:45,901
...and now you're here,
and I have this ring on my finger.

686
00:56:46,361 --> 00:56:48,987
Which makes you uncomfortable too?

687
00:56:49,948 --> 00:56:54,451
The last time I checked,
we weren't on this track.

688
00:56:54,786 --> 00:56:56,829
When was the last time
you checked?

689
00:57:17,851 --> 00:57:19,143
Bill....

690
00:57:20,687 --> 00:57:22,646
The name is Paul.

691
00:57:29,070 --> 00:57:32,364
I'm not saying no to you.

692
00:57:32,532 --> 00:57:34,867
No, you're not saying anything.

693
00:57:36,453 --> 00:57:38,495
You never really do.

694
00:57:58,391 --> 00:58:03,020
If you need me for anything else,
my number's on the refrigerator.

695
00:58:03,188 --> 00:58:05,856
I feel so guilty
leaving you alone after--

696
00:58:06,024 --> 00:58:08,567
I'm fine. I have lots of research
to do.

697
00:58:08,735 --> 00:58:12,321
That's right.
Throw yourself into work.

698
00:58:12,614 --> 00:58:13,780
I'll be back...

699
00:58:14,657 --> 00:58:16,492
...on the 2nd.

700
00:58:17,452 --> 00:58:19,328
See you next year.

701
00:58:19,496 --> 00:58:21,288
1954!

702
00:58:56,616 --> 00:58:59,576
Sunflowers. Vincent van Gogh. 1888.

703
00:58:59,744 --> 00:59:03,205
He painted what he felt,
not what he saw.

704
00:59:03,373 --> 00:59:06,792
People didn't understand. To them,
it seemed childlike and crude.

705
00:59:06,960 --> 00:59:10,170
It took years for them to recognize
his actual technique...

706
00:59:10,338 --> 00:59:14,216
...to see the way his brush strokes
seemed to make the night sky move.

707
00:59:14,384 --> 00:59:18,136
Yet, he never sold a painting
in his lifetime.

708
00:59:18,304 --> 00:59:20,305
This is his self-portrait.

709
00:59:20,473 --> 00:59:24,726
There's no camouflage, no romance.
Honesty.

710
00:59:24,894 --> 00:59:30,274
-Now, 60 years later, where is he?
-Famous?

711
00:59:30,441 --> 00:59:34,319
So famous, in fact,
that everybody has a reproduction.

712
00:59:34,487 --> 00:59:37,531
-There are post cards--
-We have the calendar.

713
00:59:37,699 --> 00:59:42,202
With the ability to reproduce art,
it is available to the masses.

714
00:59:42,370 --> 00:59:44,663
No one needs to own
a van Gogh original.

715
00:59:44,831 --> 00:59:47,791
We do. In the Newport house.

716
00:59:48,585 --> 00:59:51,670
But it's small. Tiny.

717
00:59:55,174 --> 00:59:58,427
They can paint their own.
Van Gogh in a box, ladies.

718
00:59:58,595 --> 01:00:01,680
The newest form
of mass-distributed art:

719
01:00:01,848 --> 01:00:03,432
Paint by numbers.

720
01:00:03,600 --> 01:00:06,643
"Now everyone can be van Gogh.
It's so easy.

721
01:00:06,811 --> 01:00:09,062
Just follow the simple instructions...

722
01:00:09,230 --> 01:00:12,190
...and in minutes, you're on
your way to being an artist."

723
01:00:13,234 --> 01:00:15,193
Van Gogh by numbers?

724
01:00:15,361 --> 01:00:19,031
Ironic, isn't it? Look at what
we have done to the man...

725
01:00:19,198 --> 01:00:22,826
...who refused to conform
his ideals to popular taste.

726
01:00:22,994 --> 01:00:26,038
Who refused to compromise
his integrity.

727
01:00:26,205 --> 01:00:31,793
We have put him in a tiny box
and asked you to copy him.

728
01:00:32,378 --> 01:00:34,087
So the choice is yours, ladies.

729
01:00:34,255 --> 01:00:38,717
You can conform to what
other people expect or you can--

730
01:00:38,885 --> 01:00:41,845
I know. Be ourselves.

731
01:00:42,805 --> 01:00:44,723
You're a sight for sore eyes.

732
01:00:44,891 --> 01:00:49,186
I would've been on time but, silly me,
I thought class was in the classroom.

733
01:00:49,354 --> 01:00:53,148
Glad you could join us, Mrs. Jones.
We thought we'd lost you.

734
01:00:53,399 --> 01:00:55,984
-There's an unwritten rule for marr--
-Don't bother.

735
01:00:56,152 --> 01:01:00,155
Since your wedding, you've missed
six classes, a paper and your midterm.

736
01:01:00,323 --> 01:01:03,784
Well, thank God I didn't miss
the paint-by-numbers lecture.

737
01:01:04,077 --> 01:01:07,621
I was on my honeymoon
and then I had to set up house.

738
01:01:07,789 --> 01:01:10,832
-What does she expect?
-Attendance.

739
01:01:12,460 --> 01:01:14,461
Most of the faculty turn their heads...

740
01:01:14,629 --> 01:01:17,547
...when the married students
miss a class or two.

741
01:01:17,715 --> 01:01:19,716
Then why not get married
as freshmen?

742
01:01:19,884 --> 01:01:24,971
That way you could graduate without
actually ever stepping foot on campus.

743
01:01:26,265 --> 01:01:30,560
Don't disregard our traditions
just because you're subversive.

744
01:01:32,021 --> 01:01:34,815
Don't disrespect this class
just because you're married.

745
01:01:34,982 --> 01:01:37,442
Don't disrespect me
just because you're not.

746
01:01:37,610 --> 01:01:40,779
Come to class, do the work,
or I'll fail you.

747
01:01:40,947 --> 01:01:44,074
If you fail me,
there will be consequences.

748
01:01:45,368 --> 01:01:49,079
-Are you threatening me?
-I'm educating you.

749
01:01:49,539 --> 01:01:51,373
That's my job.

750
01:02:32,999 --> 01:02:37,627
Miss Watson!

751
01:02:40,339 --> 01:02:41,506
What's this?

752
01:02:41,674 --> 01:02:45,677
Every year, the ARs nominate a
member of the faculty to be our guest.

753
01:02:45,845 --> 01:02:47,220
The what?

754
01:02:47,388 --> 01:02:50,974
You'll see. Come by tonight at 5:00.

755
01:02:53,811 --> 01:02:57,522
Adam's Ribs. A very secret society.

756
01:02:57,690 --> 01:02:59,024
Wait here.

757
01:03:07,700 --> 01:03:10,994
First, the oath.
Please raise both hands.

758
01:03:12,663 --> 01:03:15,832
Do you swear not to repeat what
you see, hear or smell tonight?

759
01:03:16,000 --> 01:03:17,501
-Smell?
-Keep your hands up!

760
01:03:17,668 --> 01:03:19,753
Yes, smell.

761
01:03:20,046 --> 01:03:21,463
I do.

762
01:03:39,482 --> 01:03:41,817
It'll only burn for a second.

763
01:03:41,984 --> 01:03:43,568
Go on.

764
01:03:53,788 --> 01:03:59,543
And now that you've taken the oath,
we get to ask you whatever we want.

765
01:03:59,710 --> 01:04:03,922
-Oh, is that how it works?
-And you have to answer.

766
01:04:04,465 --> 01:04:05,590
Who invited her?

767
01:04:05,758 --> 01:04:07,968
You're in time
for truth or consequences.

768
01:04:08,135 --> 01:04:11,471
I go first. Why aren't you married?

769
01:04:13,683 --> 01:04:15,225
Well....

770
01:04:17,061 --> 01:04:18,687
That's poisonous.

771
01:04:18,855 --> 01:04:22,607
I'm not married because...

772
01:04:23,025 --> 01:04:24,609
...I'm not.

773
01:04:25,486 --> 01:04:29,948
I was engaged to Patrick Watts.

774
01:04:30,283 --> 01:04:33,869
Everybody called him Leo,
and I never knew why.

775
01:04:34,036 --> 01:04:38,999
He was the first person
that I ever danced with...

776
01:04:39,166 --> 01:04:44,087
...or smoked with,
got incredibly drunk with and--

777
01:04:44,255 --> 01:04:47,632
Well, a lot of first things.

778
01:04:49,051 --> 01:04:53,263
We were 18 and getting married,
Christmas of ‘41.

779
01:04:54,015 --> 01:04:57,976
Then Pearl Harbor happened
and everything changed.

780
01:04:58,144 --> 01:05:02,314
Everybody changed.
And by June, he was sent overseas.

781
01:05:02,481 --> 01:05:04,316
-Did he come back?
-Yes.

782
01:05:04,483 --> 01:05:07,152
-Was he changed?
-They both were.

783
01:05:10,573 --> 01:05:12,157
I'm sorry.

784
01:05:12,867 --> 01:05:15,535
-Your parents?
-Yeah.

785
01:05:17,496 --> 01:05:19,414
After the war...

786
01:05:20,124 --> 01:05:24,544
...they didn't know each other
anymore, didn't like each other.

787
01:05:26,505 --> 01:05:29,507
He left. He got a whole new family.

788
01:05:31,302 --> 01:05:32,844
Divorce.

789
01:05:33,012 --> 01:05:34,054
What?

790
01:05:34,221 --> 01:05:38,224
Yeah. First on my block.
That's a city block.

791
01:05:40,645 --> 01:05:44,898
People change. Things happen.
It's the same with me and Leo.

792
01:05:45,316 --> 01:05:47,984
He went off and married
someone else.

793
01:05:48,277 --> 01:05:51,780
-And I got to go to graduate school.
-UCLA, right?

794
01:05:51,948 --> 01:05:55,158
-Which is in Hollywood?
-It's close.

795
01:05:55,326 --> 01:06:00,538
Anyway, aren't you gonna tell
everybody about, you know...

796
01:06:01,207 --> 01:06:03,875
-...your big news?
-What are you talking about?

797
01:06:04,085 --> 01:06:07,462
-She got engaged over Christmas!
-Congratulations!

798
01:06:07,630 --> 01:06:10,715
I'm sorry to blab.
It's just so romantic.

799
01:06:10,883 --> 01:06:12,634
How fantastic!

800
01:06:13,386 --> 01:06:15,136
We split up.

801
01:06:17,556 --> 01:06:20,850
-What?
-We split up.

802
01:06:22,687 --> 01:06:24,312
Well, that was fast.

803
01:06:24,480 --> 01:06:27,816
Well, not every relationship
is meant for marriage.

804
01:06:27,984 --> 01:06:31,236
-Some are strictly affairs?
-Bill Dunbar.

805
01:06:31,404 --> 01:06:34,614
He'd be an affair. Let's talk
about that, Miss Watson.

806
01:06:34,782 --> 01:06:36,825
You don't believe in withholding,
do you?

807
01:06:36,993 --> 01:06:41,705
No. I do, however, believe in manners.
But for you, I'll make an exception.

808
01:06:41,872 --> 01:06:45,750
That's what we're supposed to do
for married students. Right, Betty?

809
01:06:46,293 --> 01:06:50,547
Professor Dunbar and I
are not having an affair.

810
01:06:50,715 --> 01:06:52,549
Did you have one
with William Holden?

811
01:06:52,717 --> 01:06:54,759
-Connie!
-She asked about Bill Dunbar.

812
01:06:54,927 --> 01:06:57,762
-How did you hear that?
-Oh, it is true!

813
01:06:57,930 --> 01:06:59,222
Betty, I told you.

814
01:07:02,727 --> 01:07:05,937
-Won't you regret never marrying?
-There's still time.

815
01:07:06,105 --> 01:07:08,273
I guess I assume that I will
at some point.

816
01:07:08,441 --> 01:07:11,484
-I'm not gonna plan my life around it.
-Neither should we.

817
01:07:11,652 --> 01:07:13,486
-I didn't say that.
-You did to Joan.

818
01:07:13,654 --> 01:07:16,197
-That's what she told me.
-What are you saying?

819
01:07:16,365 --> 01:07:18,616
She knew you and Tommy
were getting engaged.

820
01:07:18,784 --> 01:07:21,202
And she practically filled out
your application.

821
01:07:21,370 --> 01:07:23,705
-I didn't say that.
-She's been accepted.

822
01:07:23,873 --> 01:07:27,167
Now she just has to figure out
a way to tell Tommy.

823
01:07:28,419 --> 01:07:31,796
Why don't you do it? You're good
at butting into people's business.

824
01:07:31,964 --> 01:07:35,508
Funny, that's what they say
about you.

825
01:07:37,511 --> 01:07:41,598
-Spencer, do I look all right?
-Yeah, fine.

826
01:07:41,766 --> 01:07:44,893
-I don't have a lot of time. Speed it up.
-Mr. Grouchy.

827
01:07:45,352 --> 01:07:48,980
-All right, here we go again.
-All right, go ahead, Louise.

828
01:07:49,940 --> 01:07:54,527
Married Wellesley girls have become
quite adept at balancing obligations.

829
01:07:54,820 --> 01:07:56,446
One hears such comments as:

830
01:07:56,614 --> 01:08:01,159
"I baste the chicken with one hand
and outline the paper with the other."

831
01:08:01,327 --> 01:08:04,537
While our mothers were called
to work for Lady Liberty...

832
01:08:04,705 --> 01:08:09,709
...it is our duty, nay, obligation
to reclaim our place in the home...

833
01:08:09,877 --> 01:08:14,464
...bearing the children that will carry
our traditions into the future.

834
01:08:14,632 --> 01:08:18,510
One must pause to consider why
Miss Katherine Watson...

835
01:08:18,677 --> 01:08:20,970
...instructor in the Art History
department...

836
01:08:21,138 --> 01:08:25,058
...has decided to declare war
on the holy sacrament of marriage.

837
01:08:25,226 --> 01:08:28,853
Her subversive and political teachings
encourage our Wellesley girls...

838
01:08:29,021 --> 01:08:32,607
...to reject the roles
they were born to fill.

839
01:08:49,583 --> 01:08:51,417
Thank you.

840
01:09:11,564 --> 01:09:13,773
Slide.

841
01:09:16,026 --> 01:09:18,486
-Contemporary art.
-That's just an advertisement.

842
01:09:18,654 --> 01:09:20,280
Quiet!

843
01:09:22,116 --> 01:09:24,784
Today you just listen.

844
01:09:26,662 --> 01:09:30,540
What will the future scholars see
when they study us?

845
01:09:32,334 --> 01:09:36,045
A portrait of women today?

846
01:09:38,841 --> 01:09:40,300
There you are, ladies.

847
01:09:41,260 --> 01:09:43,803
The perfect likeness
of a Wellesley graduate.

848
01:09:43,971 --> 01:09:49,642
Magna cum laude, doing exactly
what she was trained to do.

849
01:09:49,810 --> 01:09:51,102
Slide.

850
01:09:52,771 --> 01:09:54,063
A Rhodes scholar.

851
01:09:56,400 --> 01:10:00,278
I wonder if she recites Chaucer while
she presses her husband's shirts.

852
01:10:00,446 --> 01:10:01,696
Slide.

853
01:10:03,616 --> 01:10:07,619
Now, you physics majors can calculate
the mass and volume...

854
01:10:07,786 --> 01:10:10,580
...of every meat loaf you make.
Slide.

855
01:10:11,624 --> 01:10:14,042
A girdle to set you free.

856
01:10:14,210 --> 01:10:23,718
What does that mean?

857
01:10:24,970 --> 01:10:27,138
What does it mean?

858
01:10:31,477 --> 01:10:33,603
I give up.

859
01:10:35,648 --> 01:10:37,649
You win.

860
01:10:38,317 --> 01:10:42,612
The smartest women in the country.

861
01:10:45,241 --> 01:10:50,453
I didn't realize that
by demanding excellence...

862
01:10:50,913 --> 01:10:53,831
...I would be challenging....

863
01:10:56,085 --> 01:11:01,923
What did it say?

864
01:11:04,385 --> 01:11:07,845
"The roles you were born to fill."

865
01:11:08,722 --> 01:11:10,682
Is that right?

866
01:11:14,353 --> 01:11:16,980
The roles you were born to fill?

867
01:11:24,280 --> 01:11:26,698
It's my mistake.

868
01:11:39,712 --> 01:11:42,046
Class dismissed.

869
01:11:54,268 --> 01:11:58,771
These girls....
Are you proud, President Carr?

870
01:11:58,939 --> 01:12:02,984
-Yes, actually, I am.
-Well, you should be, I guess.

871
01:12:03,152 --> 01:12:06,571
Half of them are married.
The other half, give it a month or so.

872
01:12:06,739 --> 01:12:08,906
It's really only a matter of time.

873
01:12:09,074 --> 01:12:11,326
They're biding time
until somebody proposes!

874
01:12:11,493 --> 01:12:14,370
A hundred years ago, it was
inconceivable for a woman...

875
01:12:14,538 --> 01:12:16,039
...to be a college graduate.

876
01:12:16,206 --> 01:12:19,042
Perhaps you should look back
to see how far we've come.

877
01:12:19,209 --> 01:12:22,587
I'm sorry, from where I sit,
it's just a different kind of corset.

878
01:12:22,755 --> 01:12:26,799
-Well, we can all use a little support.
-Oh, like Amanda Armstrong?

879
01:12:26,967 --> 01:12:29,302
-She broke the law!
-According to Betty Warren.

880
01:12:29,470 --> 01:12:31,929
According to the state
of Massachusetts.

881
01:12:35,893 --> 01:12:38,102
To hell with Wellesley.
I'm done.

882
01:12:39,730 --> 01:12:42,106
Goddamn it!

883
01:12:44,401 --> 01:12:46,861
It's brilliant, really.
A perfect ruse.

884
01:12:47,029 --> 01:12:49,906
A finishing school disguised
as a college. They got me.

885
01:12:50,074 --> 01:12:51,991
-What do you expect?
-More. More.

886
01:12:52,159 --> 01:12:55,828
I thought it was a place for
tomorrow's leaders, not their wives.

887
01:12:55,996 --> 01:12:57,830
-Calm down, please.
-No, I will not!

888
01:12:57,998 --> 01:13:01,292
I've got 10 more minutes.
Meet me in my office. Meet me!

889
01:13:26,527 --> 01:13:31,864
-How you feeling?
-Stupid. Deceived.

890
01:13:32,116 --> 01:13:35,076
Really, really angry.

891
01:13:36,495 --> 01:13:41,999
Change takes time, you know?
You gotta let them catch up with you.

892
01:13:43,001 --> 01:13:45,628
Katherine, this place needs you.

893
01:13:46,171 --> 01:13:50,716
I don't know how the hell they let
you in, but I'm sure glad they did.

894
01:13:53,804 --> 01:13:58,641
The things I said to President Carr.
She'll never let me back in.

895
01:13:58,809 --> 01:14:00,560
She's a pretty good egg.

896
01:14:01,854 --> 01:14:04,063
What things?

897
01:14:04,231 --> 01:14:06,315
Don't worry.

898
01:14:06,733 --> 01:14:08,568
Time will heal it.

899
01:14:08,735 --> 01:14:12,738
Unless, of course, you want to go back
to California and that fiancé of yours.

900
01:14:14,199 --> 01:14:17,285
We're not engaged. Thanks.

901
01:14:17,453 --> 01:14:20,663
Sorry I interrupted your class.

902
01:14:30,883 --> 01:14:32,508
Katherine!

903
01:14:36,763 --> 01:14:41,225
I wanted to give you this.
It was for Christmas, and....

904
01:14:41,477 --> 01:14:44,353
Then I met your man, so I....

905
01:15:00,704 --> 01:15:03,206
The Sistine Chapel.

906
01:15:03,874 --> 01:15:05,666
David.

907
01:15:06,251 --> 01:15:09,337
Venus de Milo. And...

908
01:15:10,130 --> 01:15:11,839
...Mona Lisa.

909
01:15:14,593 --> 01:15:16,594
Thank you.

910
01:15:24,478 --> 01:15:27,730
This is gonna be
a three-in-one shot. Ready?

911
01:15:29,691 --> 01:15:32,693
You didn't even try.

912
01:15:34,279 --> 01:15:37,573
This game is probably a no-no
in the Better Homes and Gardens.

913
01:15:37,741 --> 01:15:41,911
Not that I have ever been in the
better homes and gardens. Have you?

914
01:15:42,079 --> 01:15:44,247
No. No, I'm just an old soldier.

915
01:15:44,414 --> 01:15:48,167
They don't invite us.

916
01:15:48,335 --> 01:15:50,461
What do they say about them though?

917
01:15:50,629 --> 01:15:53,339
-Old soldiers never die. We just--
-They just...

918
01:15:53,507 --> 01:15:56,968
...become philandering
Italian professors.

919
01:15:57,135 --> 01:15:59,303
-That was below the belt.
-That was unkind.

920
01:15:59,471 --> 01:16:00,888
Deeply unkind.

921
01:16:15,529 --> 01:16:18,990
You know, I don't know how I feel
about being a rebound.

922
01:16:19,157 --> 01:16:23,369
-I'll leave.
-No, you don't have to do that.

923
01:16:23,537 --> 01:16:25,830
I'll get used to it.

924
01:16:26,039 --> 01:16:29,333
-What are you doing?
-Getting dressed.

925
01:16:29,501 --> 01:16:33,462
I had fun, and I'm not looking
for anything serious.

926
01:16:33,630 --> 01:16:36,340
Why don't you come down here
and we can discuss it?

927
01:16:36,508 --> 01:16:40,761
But as long as we're doing
whatever it is that we're doing--

928
01:16:40,929 --> 01:16:43,764
Which we did pretty well,
don't you think?

929
01:16:47,019 --> 01:16:48,644
No students.

930
01:16:50,439 --> 01:16:52,857
I'm serious.

931
01:16:53,400 --> 01:16:58,613
I don't want to teach a class wondering
why a girl is wearing my perfume.

932
01:17:00,449 --> 01:17:03,534
-Katherine, I--
-I need your word.

933
01:17:07,205 --> 01:17:08,706
All right, you have my word.

934
01:17:11,460 --> 01:17:13,753
-Okay.
-Now can we change the subject?

935
01:17:13,920 --> 01:17:17,632
Talk about something
a little more interesting?

936
01:17:17,799 --> 01:17:20,926
Like breakfast.

937
01:17:21,386 --> 01:17:23,721
You know, I make
a mean blueberry pancake.

938
01:17:23,889 --> 01:17:25,806
I just put that shoe on.

939
01:17:25,974 --> 01:17:28,601
I don't think I know you
well enough for breakfast.

940
01:17:28,769 --> 01:17:30,478
I don't know about that.

941
01:17:34,191 --> 01:17:36,233
So how does a guy
get to know you better?

942
01:17:36,401 --> 01:17:40,404
Well, let me--
That's a good idea.

943
01:17:48,205 --> 01:17:50,665
Is that a battle wound you have?

944
01:17:54,503 --> 01:17:58,631
I'm a sucker for war stories
in Romance languages.

945
01:17:58,799 --> 01:18:01,133
-Well, that's pretty easy.
-Yeah?

946
01:18:02,928 --> 01:18:08,933
I was in a village... called San Remo...

947
01:18:11,019 --> 01:18:14,939
...the Krauts pounded us hard...

948
01:18:15,107 --> 01:18:16,649
...me and Stan...

949
01:18:16,900 --> 01:18:21,612
...you remember Stan? We were the
only two left from our platoon.

950
01:18:21,905 --> 01:18:29,245
We heard a distant cry coming
from an abandoned shed.

951
01:18:30,038 --> 01:18:31,622
When night fell...

952
01:18:32,290 --> 01:18:33,624
A trap it was?

953
01:18:36,962 --> 01:18:38,462
Remember...

954
01:18:39,256 --> 01:18:41,799
...invert your nouns and
your verbs.

955
01:19:06,491 --> 01:19:08,117
-Hungry?
-Famished.

956
01:19:08,285 --> 01:19:11,370
Don't say we can live on love.
That's how I missed breakfast.

957
01:19:15,625 --> 01:19:17,460
What's the matter?

958
01:19:18,795 --> 01:19:22,214
-What's the matter?
-Phillip and Vanessa McIntyre.

959
01:19:22,382 --> 01:19:25,009
-Parents of a friend.
-You wanna say a quick hello?

960
01:19:25,177 --> 01:19:28,179
No! No, I'll be trapped.

961
01:19:28,346 --> 01:19:30,097
Damn it!

962
01:19:33,226 --> 01:19:35,895
-Could you seat us in the bar?
-We're only serving...

963
01:19:36,062 --> 01:19:39,148
...in the front part of the restaurant
this afternoon.

964
01:19:42,986 --> 01:19:45,654
Miss! Miss Stone.

965
01:19:45,822 --> 01:19:50,493
This has been the most romantic
weekend I may ever have.

966
01:19:50,660 --> 01:19:54,789
Ever. And all that's standing
between right now and perfection...

967
01:19:55,040 --> 01:19:57,291
...are the McIntyres over there.

968
01:19:57,459 --> 01:20:02,713
Now, with the competition out there,
a girl's got to be able to move...

969
01:20:02,881 --> 01:20:05,633
...a few mountains
every once in a while.

970
01:20:05,801 --> 01:20:09,553
-I could use all the help I could get.
-Come this way.

971
01:20:14,267 --> 01:20:18,437
-I fixed it. No more McIntyres.
-Thank you. Thank you.

972
01:20:20,732 --> 01:20:24,318
-Let's not talk about this.
-All right.

973
01:20:27,030 --> 01:20:29,156
Hold your breath and--

974
01:20:31,451 --> 01:20:33,494
And turn.

975
01:20:34,037 --> 01:20:37,373
Don't forget to smile.

976
01:20:38,416 --> 01:20:40,251
Arms up.

977
01:20:40,418 --> 01:20:43,087
Move together. Faster, Fran!

978
01:20:44,256 --> 01:20:47,675
-When you surface, smile.
-Come on, Connie!

979
01:20:50,303 --> 01:20:53,806
How about we have a girls' luncheon
this weekend? Just us.

980
01:20:54,641 --> 01:20:57,101
-Where's Spencer?
-Away.

981
01:20:57,269 --> 01:20:59,436
-I'm free.
-I'm busy.

982
01:20:59,604 --> 01:21:02,147
-What are you doing?
-She's dating a psychoanalyst.

983
01:21:02,315 --> 01:21:04,108
-Oh, really?
-Who's married.

984
01:21:04,276 --> 01:21:06,861
-Giselle!
-Sorry, it slipped.

985
01:21:07,028 --> 01:21:09,405
-Are you in, Connie?
-I'll check with Charlie.

986
01:21:09,573 --> 01:21:12,491
-Who?
-Charlie Stewart. Your cousin.

987
01:21:12,659 --> 01:21:14,743
You're kidding?

988
01:21:17,080 --> 01:21:19,790
We spent last weekend at the Cape.

989
01:21:19,958 --> 01:21:23,627
-A little hideaway he knew about.
-Operative word, "hide."

990
01:21:23,795 --> 01:21:27,131
Men take women to the Cape in
the winter when they're embarrassed.

991
01:21:27,299 --> 01:21:30,467
-He's using you.
-He's not using you if you wanna go.

992
01:21:30,635 --> 01:21:32,344
Come here. Don't listen to her.

993
01:21:33,096 --> 01:21:35,973
I love you, and I swear
I'm not saying this to hurt you.

994
01:21:36,141 --> 01:21:40,185
Charlie's promised to Deb McIntyre.
She wears his pin.

995
01:21:40,979 --> 01:21:44,732
-Giselle, you know it's true.
-I don't know anything about a pin.

996
01:21:44,900 --> 01:21:46,901
McIntyre?

997
01:21:47,319 --> 01:21:50,195
Are her parents named
Phillip and Vanessa?

998
01:21:50,822 --> 01:21:52,656
You know them?

999
01:21:54,326 --> 01:21:56,118
Only from a distance.

1000
01:22:08,965 --> 01:22:12,760
-Good morning. Good morning.
-Goodbye. Good morning.

1001
01:22:12,928 --> 01:22:15,429
Okay, wait. No, we have an audience.
Don't look.

1002
01:22:18,016 --> 01:22:20,184
No, I don't--
No. Stop it. Put me down.

1003
01:22:20,352 --> 01:22:22,686
Put me down.
You're not coming in this house.

1004
01:22:22,854 --> 01:22:26,190
You are not coming
in this house. No.

1005
01:22:26,358 --> 01:22:28,150
-Bye. Good morning.
-Good morning.

1006
01:22:28,318 --> 01:22:30,277
Go away. Go away.

1007
01:22:37,369 --> 01:22:42,289
How can you date a man like that?

1008
01:22:43,416 --> 01:22:46,293
What if you're wrong about him?

1009
01:22:46,461 --> 01:22:48,629
What if I'm not?

1010
01:22:53,802 --> 01:22:56,261
Coffee's cold.

1011
01:23:17,450 --> 01:23:18,951
Row!

1012
01:23:19,119 --> 01:23:20,536
Ride the plank!

1013
01:23:20,704 --> 01:23:22,913
Row!

1014
01:23:44,936 --> 01:23:47,312
Are your ears burning?

1015
01:23:48,023 --> 01:23:51,108
I think the feet go first
when they set the stake on fire.

1016
01:23:51,276 --> 01:23:54,028
"What do you say, Edward?
Should we have her back?"

1017
01:23:54,195 --> 01:23:56,447
"She's got rather nice legs."

1018
01:23:56,614 --> 01:23:58,699
-Who's to say I wanna come back?
-What...

1019
01:23:58,867 --> 01:24:01,660
...leave me here with all these girls?

1020
01:24:11,713 --> 01:24:14,006
You'd think someone
would notice empty trays.

1021
01:24:14,174 --> 01:24:17,551
You are good. You remind me
of myself when I was your age.

1022
01:24:32,484 --> 01:24:34,401
Cheers.

1023
01:24:34,652 --> 01:24:38,489
He's positively vomititious, Giselle.

1024
01:24:38,823 --> 01:24:41,450
Don't be so sentimental, Connie.

1025
01:24:42,619 --> 01:24:45,913
It was a fling. It's fine.
It was nothing.

1026
01:24:54,881 --> 01:24:56,840
Hi!

1027
01:24:58,009 --> 01:24:59,968
Pleasure to meet you.

1028
01:25:05,683 --> 01:25:08,185
Would you excuse me?
I'll be right back.

1029
01:25:09,104 --> 01:25:12,356
Connie! Connie!
Excuse me. Sorry. Connie!

1030
01:25:12,524 --> 01:25:15,400
Charlie. It's been a while.

1031
01:25:15,568 --> 01:25:18,445
Yeah. How are you?

1032
01:25:18,696 --> 01:25:21,365
I'm fine, thank you. You? Deb?

1033
01:25:22,200 --> 01:25:25,869
-Pardon?
-Deb. Your girlfriend.

1034
01:25:26,037 --> 01:25:27,871
With the very large--

1035
01:25:28,039 --> 01:25:32,251
With the very large teeth.

1036
01:25:32,418 --> 01:25:36,380
-Oh, did you think I didn't know?
-Connie.

1037
01:25:36,548 --> 01:25:40,551
Deb and I broke it off last summer.
That's Miranda.

1038
01:25:40,718 --> 01:25:46,348
We started seeing each other when
you stopped returning my calls...

1039
01:25:46,516 --> 01:25:49,476
...or answering any of my letters.

1040
01:25:55,191 --> 01:25:56,692
Okay, maybe I should go.

1041
01:26:16,129 --> 01:26:18,630
Have you seen Spencer?

1042
01:26:25,388 --> 01:26:28,223
But I did see Charlie Stewart.

1043
01:26:29,767 --> 01:26:33,562
And he told me that he and Deb
broke up last summer.

1044
01:26:34,272 --> 01:26:38,567
And you told me they were together
when he invited me to the Cape.

1045
01:26:38,735 --> 01:26:41,778
Oh, Connie.
I don't keep track of his dates.

1046
01:26:41,946 --> 01:26:46,450
They've been on-again, off-again
for the past few years.

1047
01:26:46,618 --> 01:26:50,787
No, apparently they've been
off-again for a while. For quite a while.

1048
01:26:50,955 --> 01:26:53,916
-So?
-So you made me believe...

1049
01:26:54,083 --> 01:26:56,710
...that he was hiding me.

1050
01:26:58,880 --> 01:27:01,215
Either way...

1051
01:27:01,382 --> 01:27:04,218
...why couldn't you let me be happy?

1052
01:27:10,475 --> 01:27:12,392
Betty.

1053
01:27:35,875 --> 01:27:38,168
And we switch.

1054
01:27:40,088 --> 01:27:42,881
-Wonder teacher.
-Tommy.

1055
01:27:45,218 --> 01:27:48,762
-How's Harvard?
-Oh, not too bad. Not too bad.

1056
01:27:48,930 --> 01:27:51,431
-Congratulate me.
-You set a date?

1057
01:27:53,977 --> 01:27:56,019
No, nothing official. Yet.

1058
01:27:56,187 --> 01:27:59,189
I meant, I got into Penn. Grad school.

1059
01:27:59,649 --> 01:28:02,609
Congratulations. What about Yale?

1060
01:28:03,194 --> 01:28:08,490
Yale? Oh, you mean Joanie.
Yeah. How about that, huh?

1061
01:28:08,700 --> 01:28:11,285
She is some girl.

1062
01:28:11,452 --> 01:28:14,037
-She's terrific.
-Yeah.

1063
01:28:14,205 --> 01:28:19,418
Just the fact that she got in.
I mean, she will always have that.

1064
01:28:19,585 --> 01:28:23,547
Thanks to you. Miss Watson,
you've been real swell to her.

1065
01:28:23,715 --> 01:28:26,300
We both appreciate it.

1066
01:28:27,719 --> 01:28:31,013
I'm sorry. "The fact that she got in,"
what does that mean?

1067
01:28:31,180 --> 01:28:34,474
Well, she'll be in Philadelphia.

1068
01:28:34,642 --> 01:28:36,101
With me.

1069
01:28:39,397 --> 01:28:43,483
Well, that's an awful long commute
to get dinner on the table by 5:00.

1070
01:28:44,068 --> 01:28:47,863
-And we switch.
-Thank you.

1071
01:28:53,911 --> 01:28:55,746
Excuse me.

1072
01:28:55,997 --> 01:28:59,374
Excuse me. Have you seen Spencer?
I can't find him anywhere.

1073
01:29:03,087 --> 01:29:06,923
Actually, Spencer asked me
to take you home.

1074
01:29:07,091 --> 01:29:11,219
-He has this meeting--
-In New York.

1075
01:29:11,596 --> 01:29:13,472
Thank you.

1076
01:29:42,126 --> 01:29:45,587
-Honey, what are you doing here?
-I'm staying the night.

1077
01:29:45,755 --> 01:29:49,549
-Spencer won't mind?
-Spencer won't notice.

1078
01:29:49,717 --> 01:29:55,347
-He's in New York again. Working.
-He's working hard for both of you.

1079
01:29:55,515 --> 01:29:59,351
Don't lie for him, Mother.
He does it so well for himself.

1080
01:29:59,519 --> 01:30:02,020
You're going to turn around,
go home...

1081
01:30:02,188 --> 01:30:04,481
...fix your face
and wait for your husband.

1082
01:30:04,649 --> 01:30:07,859
This is the bargain you made,
Elizabeth. We all did.

1083
01:30:08,027 --> 01:30:10,487
So you're not gonna let me
stay in my own house?

1084
01:30:10,655 --> 01:30:12,823
Spencer's house is your house now.

1085
01:30:12,990 --> 01:30:16,952
Believe me, it's for your own good.

1086
01:30:34,011 --> 01:30:36,805
Miss Watson. Come in.

1087
01:30:37,223 --> 01:30:40,016
Seven law schools within 45 minutes
of Philadelphia.

1088
01:30:40,184 --> 01:30:42,894
You can study and get dinner
on the table by 5:00.

1089
01:30:43,062 --> 01:30:45,897
-It's too late.
-No. Some accept late admissions.

1090
01:30:46,065 --> 01:30:48,233
-I was upset at first.
-Joan, the guests.

1091
01:30:48,401 --> 01:30:51,361
When Tommy told me that he got
accepted to Penn, I thought:

1092
01:30:51,529 --> 01:30:54,364
"Her fate is sealed.
How can she throw it all away?"

1093
01:30:54,532 --> 01:30:58,326
I realized you won't have to. You
could bake your cake and eat it too.

1094
01:30:58,494 --> 01:31:00,704
We're married.

1095
01:31:02,039 --> 01:31:03,874
We eloped over the weekend.

1096
01:31:04,041 --> 01:31:06,793
Turned out he was petrified
of a big ceremony...

1097
01:31:06,961 --> 01:31:10,046
...so we did a sort of
spur-of-the-moment thing.

1098
01:31:10,214 --> 01:31:12,174
Very romantic.

1099
01:31:12,341 --> 01:31:14,092
Look.

1100
01:31:22,518 --> 01:31:24,644
It's beautiful.

1101
01:31:27,648 --> 01:31:32,444
It was my choice. Not to go.
He would have supported it.

1102
01:31:33,070 --> 01:31:36,656
-But you don't have to choose.
-No, I have to.

1103
01:31:36,824 --> 01:31:39,784
I want a home, a family.
It's not something I'll sacrifice.

1104
01:31:39,952 --> 01:31:43,121
No one's asking you
to sacrifice that, Joan.

1105
01:31:43,289 --> 01:31:46,041
I just want you to understand
that you can do both.

1106
01:31:46,209 --> 01:31:49,085
Think I'll wake up one day
and regret not being a lawyer?

1107
01:31:49,253 --> 01:31:50,795
Yes, I'm afraid that you will.

1108
01:31:50,963 --> 01:31:55,425
Not as much as I'd regret not having
a family. Not being there to raise them.

1109
01:31:55,593 --> 01:31:59,971
I know exactly what I'm doing, and
it doesn't make me any less smart.

1110
01:32:00,848 --> 01:32:03,892
-This must seem terrible to you.
-I didn't say that. I--

1111
01:32:04,060 --> 01:32:07,103
Sure you did. You always do.

1112
01:32:07,271 --> 01:32:10,941
You stand in class and tell us to look
beyond the image, but you don't.

1113
01:32:11,108 --> 01:32:15,028
To you, a housewife is someone who
sold her soul for a center hall colonial.

1114
01:32:15,196 --> 01:32:18,990
She has no depth,
no intellect, no interests.

1115
01:32:21,786 --> 01:32:25,580
You're the one who said
I could do anything I wanted.

1116
01:32:25,748 --> 01:32:28,124
This is what I want.

1117
01:32:32,672 --> 01:32:34,839
Congratulations.

1118
01:32:35,007 --> 01:32:37,050
Be happy.

1119
01:32:58,114 --> 01:33:02,200
Sometimes I think you say
these things to provoke me.

1120
01:33:02,368 --> 01:33:05,328
Does your father know
that you speak this way?

1121
01:33:09,834 --> 01:33:12,669
Does your wife know you're here?

1122
01:33:50,374 --> 01:33:54,044
-Where might I find Charlie Stewart?
-Charlie Stewart? C1744.

1123
01:33:54,211 --> 01:33:57,464
-But it's too late. You'll have to leave.
-All right.

1124
01:34:02,094 --> 01:34:04,262
You can't go up there!

1125
01:34:07,516 --> 01:34:09,392
What is going on here?

1126
01:34:09,560 --> 01:34:11,895
There's no women allowed
in the dorms!

1127
01:34:13,731 --> 01:34:16,900
Can you please not interrupt me?!

1128
01:34:18,444 --> 01:34:23,573
Connie! What are you doing here?

1129
01:34:25,493 --> 01:34:26,868
Well, I saw....

1130
01:34:27,036 --> 01:34:29,120
I saw Big Teeth with...

1131
01:34:29,288 --> 01:34:32,916
...Kevin Tawil looking pretty cozy,
and I thought that maybe....

1132
01:34:33,084 --> 01:34:35,669
Maybe you two weren't....

1133
01:34:36,587 --> 01:34:40,298
-Dating?
-Yes. Thank you.

1134
01:34:40,591 --> 01:34:42,759
Hey, no girls in the dorm.

1135
01:34:46,764 --> 01:34:49,974
I know I have made mistakes.
A ton.

1136
01:34:50,559 --> 01:34:53,395
But I never make them twice.

1137
01:35:12,707 --> 01:35:14,249
And?

1138
01:35:14,792 --> 01:35:20,422
And it was perfect. Romantic.
We stayed up all night talking.

1139
01:35:20,589 --> 01:35:22,924
You're late. What happened
to Sunday brunch?

1140
01:35:23,092 --> 01:35:26,302
We stayed up all night too.
Not talking.

1141
01:35:26,929 --> 01:35:30,265
The psychoanalyst? Again.

1142
01:35:32,143 --> 01:35:35,228
-Divine exhaustion.
-He's married.

1143
01:35:35,396 --> 01:35:37,188
He's not married like
you and Tommy.

1144
01:35:37,356 --> 01:35:38,815
What does that mean?

1145
01:35:38,983 --> 01:35:42,110
It means he and his wife
don't speak the same language.

1146
01:35:42,278 --> 01:35:45,029
-Spelled S-E-X.
-Does he pay you?

1147
01:35:45,197 --> 01:35:46,740
For sex?

1148
01:35:47,074 --> 01:35:49,617
At the rate you're going,
you could make a fortune.

1149
01:35:50,161 --> 01:35:53,538
-Betty.
-Everyone thinks so.

1150
01:35:53,706 --> 01:35:57,417
Do you know what they say?
They say you're a whore.

1151
01:35:57,918 --> 01:36:01,963
Once they've all sampled you,
they'll toss you aside like a used rag.

1152
01:36:02,131 --> 01:36:04,966
-Betty, stop now.
-The men you love don't want you.

1153
01:36:05,134 --> 01:36:08,511
-Your father doesn't want you.
-I'm gonna meet you downstairs.

1154
01:36:08,679 --> 01:36:10,680
-Professor Dunbar.
-Betty, that's enough.

1155
01:36:10,848 --> 01:36:13,600
Everybody knows that you hide
outside his house.

1156
01:36:13,809 --> 01:36:17,479
It must be torturous running after
a man who doesn't care about you...

1157
01:36:17,897 --> 01:36:21,191
...who's in love with someone else,
who hates you.

1158
01:36:21,358 --> 01:36:23,693
-He hates you!
-Betty.

1159
01:36:23,861 --> 01:36:25,820
And it hurts! No!

1160
01:36:25,988 --> 01:36:28,990
-Get off me!
-Quiet!

1161
01:36:38,042 --> 01:36:40,043
Oh, God.

1162
01:36:41,879 --> 01:36:45,173
-He doesn't want me.
-Okay.

1163
01:36:45,382 --> 01:36:48,468
He doesn't sleep with me. He--

1164
01:36:48,761 --> 01:36:50,303
I know.

1165
01:37:02,733 --> 01:37:06,903
I say no. I mean, it's not fair to her.

1166
01:37:07,071 --> 01:37:10,740
-She's not happy.
-Don't forget about her outburst.

1167
01:37:10,908 --> 01:37:13,034
Oh, that's right.

1168
01:37:13,285 --> 01:37:16,913
Dr. Staunton, will you tell the
alumnae the figures you have?

1169
01:37:17,081 --> 01:37:20,041
Enrollment for her class next year...

1170
01:37:20,209 --> 01:37:24,712
...is the highest
the department's had. Ever.

1171
01:37:25,714 --> 01:37:28,383
She'd have to promise
to turn in her lesson plans.

1172
01:37:28,759 --> 01:37:32,679
In advance. And they'll need
to be approved.

1173
01:37:32,847 --> 01:37:34,848
She'll never agree to that.

1174
01:37:44,066 --> 01:37:47,193
Bill, the brochures
for Europe arrived.

1175
01:37:51,699 --> 01:37:53,616
Surprise!

1176
01:37:53,951 --> 01:37:56,619
Sir, I'm so sorry!

1177
01:37:56,787 --> 01:37:59,289
-Who are you?
-I'm Stanley Sher...

1178
01:37:59,456 --> 01:38:02,083
...a friend of Bill's.
We were in the 37th together.

1179
01:38:02,251 --> 01:38:05,003
-Okay.
-You must be Katherine.

1180
01:38:05,170 --> 01:38:07,171
I am.

1181
01:38:07,339 --> 01:38:10,884
He told me you were a looker, but....

1182
01:38:12,845 --> 01:38:16,806
-I'll wait for you downstairs, Stanley.
-Okey-dokey.

1183
01:38:20,728 --> 01:38:22,979
So you and Bill
were in the war together?

1184
01:38:23,147 --> 01:38:26,691
Yep. Now he's some
fancy teacher...

1185
01:38:26,859 --> 01:38:29,235
...and I'm in air conditioning.

1186
01:38:29,403 --> 01:38:30,904
Forget the A-bomb.

1187
01:38:31,822 --> 01:38:34,741
Freon. It's gonna change
the good old U.S. of A.

1188
01:38:35,117 --> 01:38:39,120
Open the entire West to development.
I'm based in El Paso.

1189
01:38:39,538 --> 01:38:42,415
-Oh, thank you.
-And cheers.

1190
01:38:45,294 --> 01:38:47,754
Well, you've come a long way
from San Remo.

1191
01:38:47,922 --> 01:38:49,839
Yes, sirree.

1192
01:38:50,466 --> 01:38:52,634
That in California?

1193
01:38:53,344 --> 01:38:54,844
Italy.

1194
01:38:55,012 --> 01:38:58,431
-Where you two were stationed.
-Italy?

1195
01:38:58,599 --> 01:39:01,059
Well, somebody's been
pulling your long leg.

1196
01:39:01,226 --> 01:39:05,480
We were stationed at the Army
Language Institute on Long Island.

1197
01:39:05,648 --> 01:39:09,567
Closest we ever got to Italy
was the baked ziti at Mama Leone's.

1198
01:39:09,735 --> 01:39:12,695
I don't think Bill's
ever been to Europe.

1199
01:39:12,863 --> 01:39:15,031
I sure as hell haven't.

1200
01:39:15,407 --> 01:39:16,699
Cheers.

1201
01:39:26,877 --> 01:39:28,795
Katherine!

1202
01:39:32,800 --> 01:39:34,550
You okay?

1203
01:39:35,511 --> 01:39:37,553
I met Stan.

1204
01:39:38,514 --> 01:39:39,764
Hell of a nice guy.

1205
01:39:39,932 --> 01:39:41,724
-Yeah.
-What a talker.

1206
01:39:41,892 --> 01:39:43,810
Yeah.

1207
01:39:43,978 --> 01:39:47,063
We go back a long way.

1208
01:39:47,231 --> 01:39:49,732
All the way to Long Island.

1209
01:39:53,696 --> 01:39:55,905
Secret's out.

1210
01:39:57,408 --> 01:40:00,159
What an incredible lie.

1211
01:40:03,247 --> 01:40:06,833
I spoke the language, I had the
uniform, people just assumed things.

1212
01:40:07,001 --> 01:40:11,504
I didn't correct them.
I guess I should've done.

1213
01:40:12,756 --> 01:40:15,133
I guess you should've done.

1214
01:40:16,885 --> 01:40:19,762
Katherine, look, I'm sorry, okay?

1215
01:40:20,431 --> 01:40:22,432
I made a mistake.

1216
01:40:22,599 --> 01:40:24,517
Just give me a chance...

1217
01:40:24,685 --> 01:40:27,228
...to straighten things out, okay?

1218
01:40:30,482 --> 01:40:33,276
Why couldn't you just be honest?

1219
01:40:36,071 --> 01:40:38,156
You don't make it easy.

1220
01:40:40,325 --> 01:40:42,201
You're so perfect, you....

1221
01:40:44,288 --> 01:40:47,331
It's impossible to be honest
with you. I....

1222
01:40:48,459 --> 01:40:50,543
For you, it is.

1223
01:40:53,714 --> 01:40:57,800
Well, it's not just me, Katherine.
Joan failed you too, right?

1224
01:40:57,968 --> 01:41:01,345
-That's an awful thing to say.
-I know, but it's the truth.

1225
01:41:02,639 --> 01:41:05,475
If you want honesty,
I can be real honest.

1226
01:41:05,642 --> 01:41:08,644
You didn't come to Wellesley
to help people find their way.

1227
01:41:08,812 --> 01:41:12,356
I think you came to help people
find your way.

1228
01:41:37,007 --> 01:41:40,426
I'm not accustomed
to hunting you down.

1229
01:41:42,387 --> 01:41:44,972
Elizabeth, look at me, please.

1230
01:41:49,561 --> 01:41:52,563
I've spoken with Mrs. Jones.
There will be no divorce.

1231
01:41:53,857 --> 01:41:57,026
There's always a period
of adjustment.

1232
01:41:57,194 --> 01:42:01,030
I've assured her that you will try
for a year.

1233
01:42:03,617 --> 01:42:05,910
Look at this, Mother.

1234
01:42:10,749 --> 01:42:12,959
Spencer will try as well.

1235
01:42:13,127 --> 01:42:17,338
According to her, he's really
very upset. You should call him.

1236
01:42:17,506 --> 01:42:21,467
She's smiling. Is she happy?

1237
01:42:22,886 --> 01:42:26,722
The important thing
is not to tell anyone.

1238
01:42:26,890 --> 01:42:28,599
She looks happy.

1239
01:42:29,143 --> 01:42:31,477
So, what does it matter?

1240
01:42:32,146 --> 01:42:36,482
Don't wash your dirty laundry
in public.

1241
01:42:36,775 --> 01:42:39,402
Let me tell you something.

1242
01:42:40,696 --> 01:42:44,157
Not everything is as it seems.

1243
01:43:03,385 --> 01:43:05,928
Dear Miss Watson:

1244
01:43:06,096 --> 01:43:08,264
It is with great pleasure...

1245
01:43:08,432 --> 01:43:10,600
...that we invite you...

1246
01:43:10,767 --> 01:43:14,270
...to return as an instructor...

1247
01:43:14,438 --> 01:43:16,772
...in the Art History department...

1248
01:43:16,940 --> 01:43:22,570
...for the 1954-1955
academic year.

1249
01:43:22,738 --> 01:43:27,617
We do wish to make clear, however,
that this invitation...

1250
01:43:27,784 --> 01:43:33,247
...is absolutely conditional
upon the following:

1251
01:43:34,791 --> 01:43:36,959
Please, Nora, may we continue?

1252
01:43:37,127 --> 01:43:39,253
Number one:

1253
01:43:39,755 --> 01:43:44,967
You will teach only the syllabus
as outlined by the department chair.

1254
01:43:45,928 --> 01:43:47,637
Number two:

1255
01:43:47,804 --> 01:43:49,680
All lesson plans...

1256
01:43:49,848 --> 01:43:55,144
...must be submitted at the beginning
of every term for approval...

1257
01:43:55,312 --> 01:43:57,438
...and revision.

1258
01:43:57,606 --> 01:43:58,981
Number three:

1259
01:43:59,149 --> 01:44:03,236
You shall not provide counsel
beyond your own subject...

1260
01:44:03,403 --> 01:44:06,447
...for any student at any time.

1261
01:44:06,615 --> 01:44:08,241
And finally:

1262
01:44:08,408 --> 01:44:13,746
That you will agree to maintain
a strictly professional relationship...

1263
01:44:13,914 --> 01:44:16,499
...with all members of the faculty.

1264
01:44:16,667 --> 01:44:19,168
-Good evening, Katherine.
-Hi, Violet.

1265
01:44:19,544 --> 01:44:23,714
Assuming you accept the conditions,
we look forward to your continuing...

1266
01:44:23,882 --> 01:44:26,133
...to be a part
of our Wellesley tradition.

1267
01:44:34,893 --> 01:44:37,311
Nice party.

1268
01:44:37,479 --> 01:44:39,188
I didn't realize.

1269
01:44:40,274 --> 01:44:42,108
It was a surprise.

1270
01:44:42,317 --> 01:44:45,069
William H. Taft.
What does the letter H stand for?

1271
01:44:45,237 --> 01:44:47,697
-Howard.
-Howard. Yes!

1272
01:44:48,490 --> 01:44:50,324
Howard.

1273
01:44:51,243 --> 01:44:53,244
Oh, it doesn't matter.

1274
01:44:53,412 --> 01:44:57,290
The important thing is
that you'll be back next year.

1275
01:44:57,457 --> 01:44:59,875
Hopefully living here.

1276
01:45:02,921 --> 01:45:07,300
Well, I haven't really thought about
that. They just told me today.

1277
01:45:09,219 --> 01:45:11,512
Well, there's still plenty of time.

1278
01:45:11,888 --> 01:45:14,890
You should be celebrating.

1279
01:45:20,814 --> 01:45:22,565
You're right.

1280
01:45:24,651 --> 01:45:26,694
You're right. Let's celebrate.

1281
01:45:29,990 --> 01:45:33,784
Let's go upstairs and get gussied up
and go out dancing.

1282
01:45:36,121 --> 01:45:40,791
Silly, it's after 8:00.
Strike It Rich is on.

1283
01:45:40,959 --> 01:45:44,962
-Life isn't about Strike It Rich.
-It's a school night.

1284
01:45:45,130 --> 01:45:49,467
-So what?
-So I don't want to go. I'm happy here.

1285
01:45:51,011 --> 01:45:52,303
It's back on.

1286
01:45:55,766 --> 01:45:59,477
-Do you feel good about music....
-Oh, isn't he handsome?

1287
01:46:01,063 --> 01:46:02,980
Watch with me.

1288
01:46:04,733 --> 01:46:09,987
"You talked me into it," says this
oldie. What's the title of this one?

1289
01:46:13,283 --> 01:46:15,368
"You Made Me Love You."

1290
01:46:15,535 --> 01:46:19,246
"You Made Me Love--"
"You Made Me Love You."

1291
01:46:23,293 --> 01:46:25,961
You get all that from looking
at paint on a canvas...

1292
01:46:26,129 --> 01:46:28,839
...and it's her facial expression...

1293
01:46:29,007 --> 01:46:31,133
...her eyes that makes
you think that.

1294
01:46:31,301 --> 01:46:35,096
-I think that makes it interesting.
-The context that it comes from...

1295
01:46:35,263 --> 01:46:36,806
...affects the way we view it.

1296
01:46:36,973 --> 01:46:40,059
I think it provokes us because
it provoked the painter...

1297
01:46:40,227 --> 01:46:43,604
...and in turn, he's kind of sending
that message to us.

1298
01:46:43,772 --> 01:46:47,316
Whether or not it's a good painting
cannot be subjective.

1299
01:46:47,484 --> 01:46:50,277
I feel like I'm missing something.

1300
01:47:43,290 --> 01:47:45,374
It was Joan's idea.

1301
01:47:49,880 --> 01:47:51,964
How else will you remember us?

1302
01:48:35,217 --> 01:48:38,260
-They've invited you back.
-Do you think they made a mistake?

1303
01:48:38,428 --> 01:48:39,887
I do.

1304
01:48:44,643 --> 01:48:48,896
-Elizabeth, I don't see Spencer.
-Excuse me, Mother.

1305
01:48:49,064 --> 01:48:52,441
Miss Watson, can you help me
get in touch with your friend...

1306
01:48:52,609 --> 01:48:53,901
...in Greenwich Village?

1307
01:48:54,069 --> 01:48:56,487
What do you need
in Greenwich Village?

1308
01:48:56,655 --> 01:48:58,072
An apartment.

1309
01:48:59,574 --> 01:49:02,535
I filed for a divorce this morning.

1310
01:49:03,787 --> 01:49:06,872
And since we know
I'm not welcome at your house....

1311
01:49:08,583 --> 01:49:12,753
You remember Giselle Levy?
What did you call her?

1312
01:49:13,630 --> 01:49:16,131
"A New York kike." That's it.

1313
01:49:16,800 --> 01:49:19,593
Well, we're going to be roommates.

1314
01:49:20,679 --> 01:49:22,471
Hi.

1315
01:49:22,639 --> 01:49:26,141
-You ready?
-Yeah.

1316
01:49:38,697 --> 01:49:40,489
You okay?

1317
01:49:42,534 --> 01:49:45,452
-Greenwich Village?
-Yeah. For a while.

1318
01:49:46,246 --> 01:49:48,497
Then, who knows?

1319
01:49:48,665 --> 01:49:51,959
Maybe law school. Yale, even.

1320
01:49:54,170 --> 01:49:56,005
Well...

1321
01:49:56,673 --> 01:50:01,218
...I wouldn't want to come up against
you in any court anywhere.

1322
01:50:01,386 --> 01:50:04,096
Maybe I can drop by next year?

1323
01:50:04,264 --> 01:50:06,557
Keep you on your toes.

1324
01:50:08,685 --> 01:50:10,769
You will be here?

1325
01:50:11,521 --> 01:50:13,522
Miss Watson?

1326
01:50:19,613 --> 01:50:21,530
Dear Betty:

1327
01:50:21,698 --> 01:50:26,535
I came to Wellesley because
I wanted to make a difference.

1328
01:50:27,120 --> 01:50:28,829
But to change for others....

1329
01:50:28,997 --> 01:50:31,165
--is to lie to yourself.

1330
01:50:31,333 --> 01:50:32,625
My teacher...

1331
01:50:32,792 --> 01:50:36,170
...Katherine Watson,
lived by her own definition...

1332
01:50:36,338 --> 01:50:38,589
...and would not compromise that.

1333
01:50:38,757 --> 01:50:41,467
Not even for Wellesley.

1334
01:50:42,093 --> 01:50:44,928
I dedicate this, my last editorial...

1335
01:50:45,096 --> 01:50:47,056
...to an extraordinary woman...

1336
01:50:47,223 --> 01:50:49,141
...who lived by example...

1337
01:50:49,309 --> 01:50:53,354
...and compelled us all to see
the world through new eyes.

1338
01:50:54,564 --> 01:50:58,442
By the time you read this,
she'll be sailing to Europe...

1339
01:50:58,610 --> 01:51:01,528
...where I know she'll find new walls
to break down...

1340
01:51:01,696 --> 01:51:03,656
...and new ideas
to replace them with.

1341
01:51:03,823 --> 01:51:05,908
Hold it, everybody.

1342
01:51:11,748 --> 01:51:14,083
I've heard her called a quitter
for leaving...

1343
01:51:14,250 --> 01:51:16,710
...an aimless wanderer.

1344
01:51:16,878 --> 01:51:21,090
But not all who wander are aimless.

1345
01:51:21,257 --> 01:51:25,761
Especially not those who seek truth
beyond tradition...

1346
01:51:27,764 --> 01:51:29,973
...beyond definition...

1347
01:51:32,686 --> 01:51:34,770
...beyond the image.

1348
01:51:35,188 --> 01:51:37,690
Get the hell out of the way!

1349
01:51:47,200 --> 01:51:49,410
I'll never forget you.

1350
01:51:54,666 --> 01:51:58,919
Betty! Betty!

1351
01:52:28,616 --> 01:52:30,242
What about after this war, Lee?

1352
01:52:30,410 --> 01:52:33,162
Well, this job belongs
to some soldier.

1353
01:52:33,329 --> 01:52:34,621
When he comes back...

1354
01:52:34,789 --> 01:52:37,207
-...he can have it.
-Oh, that's swell.


